---
import asyncio

# Progress must live OUTSIDE the wires. In stateless mode the page's state
# round-trips in the client's signed snapshot, so a background task cannot
# mutate the wires of a request that was answered and forgotten. A frontmatter
# class attribute compiles to module level: one dict for the whole process,
# shared by every page instance and every background task. (A plain top-level
# JOBS = {} would NOT work -- it compiles to a per-instance attribute that is
# rebuilt on every POST.)
class Jobs:
    store = {}
    seq = 0

status = wire("idle")
progress = wire(0)
job_id = wire("")

# Literal syntax samples live in frontmatter: .wire prose cannot contain raw
# braces or a stray dollar sign -- the template parser would eat them.
POLL_SYNTAX = "@poll.every-400={tick(job_id)}"
STOP_SYNTAX = "{$if status == 'running'} ... {/if}"

def start():
    Jobs.seq += 1
    jid = "job-" + str(Jobs.seq)
    Jobs.store[jid] = 0
    job_id.value = jid
    progress.value = 0
    status.value = "running"
    asyncio.create_task(_run(jid))

async def _run(jid):
    # Stands in for a real LLM generation call. It writes ONLY to the global
    # store -- it has no request, no wires, no snapshot to touch.
    for pct in (20, 40, 60, 80, 100):
        await asyncio.sleep(0.4)
        Jobs.store[jid] = pct

def tick(jid):
    # Each poll tick READS the store into wires; that is the whole channel
    # from background work back to the page.
    progress.value = Jobs.store.get(jid, 0)
    if progress.value >= 100:
        status.value = "done"
---
<div>
    <h1>Background jobs without a WebSocket</h1>
    <p>The stateless idiom for bounded long-running work: kick off a task,
    keep its progress in a process-global store, and let a polled element read
    that store back every few hundred milliseconds.</p>

    <section id="job">
        <button id="start-btn" @click={start()}>Start generation</button>
        <p>Status: <span id="job-status">{status}</span></p>

        {$if status == 'running'}
        <div id="job-running">
            <p id="job-progress" @poll.every-400={tick(job_id)}>Generating&hellip; {progress}%</p>
            <p>The line above is the poll: <code>{POLL_SYNTAX}</code>. Every
            400&#8239;ms it dispatches one POST to the server, exactly like a
            click &mdash; watch the Network tab.</p>
        </div>
        {/if}

        {$if status == 'done'}
        <div id="job-done">
            <p>Done &mdash; {progress}%. The polled element has been removed
            from the DOM, and its timer with it. There is no
            <code>.while</code> modifier: the stop condition IS the
            conditional render <code>{STOP_SYNTAX}</code>.</p>
            <button id="run-again" @click={start()}>Run again</button>
        </div>
        {/if}
    </section>

    <section id="why">
        <h2>Why the global store?</h2>
        <p>Wires alone cannot work here. The background task runs outside any
        request, and in stateless mode the wires it would mutate live in the
        client's snapshot, not in server memory. The store is the shared
        truth; the wires are just the page's last copy of it, refreshed on
        every tick.</p>
        <p>When NOT to poll: per-token LLM streaming. Polling is for bounded
        waits where a coarse progress number every few hundred milliseconds is
        enough. True streaming wants server push &mdash; the stateful
        WebSocket tier today, SSE on the roadmap.</p>
    </section>
</div>
